IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g5-interpretation`
IMPORT `g5-nouns`

-- g5-art14-court-powers.l4: Article 14 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 -- the powers of the court over a registered charity.
-- The court's power is a permission (MAY): an unexercised permission is not a breach.  What the court is satisfied of ("it appears to the court",
-- "sees fit", "thinks desirable") is an input.  The quotations are copied mechanically from the Law's text file by srcrefs.py.
-- Who applies is a fact (a constructor of `Application to the court under Article 14`); the application is also encoded as the applicant's own
-- permission (the Commissioner, the Attorney General and, for 14(4), the charity and the person the order was directed at), followed by the court's.
-- Forks: F5.4 (misconduct in the administration), F5.9 (the first day of a period fixed by the court), F5.12 (the three purposes of 14(2)).
-- Article 17(9) lends these powers to a deregistered entity; that is G6's, which types its action with `Order of the court over a charity` (IP31).

§ `Part 4 -- Charity register`

§§ `14 Powers of court over registered charity`

§§§ `14(1) When the court may exercise the powers`

@ref Article 14(1) (src:407)
GIVEN application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(1) the application is by the Commissioner or the Attorney General` application MEANS
    CONSIDER application
    WHEN `an application by the Commissioner` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an application by the Attorney General` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Fork F5.4.  (a) "that there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity".  "Misconduct" is a defined term (Article 1, 2(10), 18(4));
-- "in the administration of the charity" is not defined.  The facts are given as they appear to the court.
-- Reading A (taken): misconduct within Article 2(10) that bears on the administration of the charity.
@ref Article 14(1)(a) (src:408)
GIVEN `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F5.4 A: misconduct within Article 2(10) that bears on the administration of the charity` `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears MEANS
        `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)`
    AND appears's `the conduct concerns the administration of the charity`

-- Reading B: any conduct that is misconduct within Article 2(10), "in the administration" adding nothing.
@ref Article 14(1)(a) (src:408)
GIVEN `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F5.4 B: any misconduct within Article 2(10)` `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` MEANS
    `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)`

-- Reading C: "misconduct" in its ordinary sense (the defined term not applied) in the administration of the charity.
@ref Article 14(1)(a) (src:408)
GIVEN appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F5.4 C: misconduct in the ordinary sense in the administration of the charity` appears MEANS
        appears's `the conduct is misconduct in the ordinary sense`
    AND appears's `the conduct concerns the administration of the charity`

@ref Article 14(1)(a) (src:408)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14`
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(1)(a) there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity` interp `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the meaning of misconduct in the administration`
    WHEN `misconduct within Article 2(10) that bears on the administration of the charity`
        THEN `F5.4 A: misconduct within Article 2(10) that bears on the administration of the charity` `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears
    WHEN `any misconduct within Article 2(10)`
        THEN `F5.4 B: any misconduct within Article 2(10)` `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)`
    WHEN `misconduct in the ordinary sense, not the defined term`
        THEN `F5.4 C: misconduct in the ordinary sense in the administration of the charity` appears

@ref Article 14(1)(b) (src:409)
GIVEN appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(1)(b) it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the property or secure its proper application` appears MEANS
    appears's `it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the charity's property or secure its proper application`

-- The gate.  Both grounds are alternatives ("or"); the application must be by the Commissioner or the Attorney General; and the charity must be a
-- registered charity ("in relation to a registered charity").
-- src:407 | (1) The court may exercise any of the powers set out in paragraph (2) in relation to a registered charity if, on an application by the Commissioner or the Attorney General, it appears to the court –
-- src:408 | (a) that there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity; or
-- src:409 | (b) that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the property of the charity or securing a proper application of that property in accordance with the charity's registered charitable purposes and registered public benefit statement.
@ref Article 14(1) (src:407-409)
@export Whether the powers of the court under Article 14(2) are exercisable over a registered charity: an application by the Commissioner or the Attorney General, and it appears to the court that there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity, or that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the charity's property or secure its proper application
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14` @desc The readings of the Law's open points; fork F5.4 decides what misconduct in the administration of the charity means
      `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is entered for the time being in the general or the restricted section of the register
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14` @desc Who applied to the court
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct in question is misconduct within Article 2(10), as it appears to the court
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)` @desc The other facts as they appear to the court: whether the conduct concerns the administration, whether it is misconduct in the ordinary sense, and whether an order is necessary or desirable to protect the property
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(1) the powers of the court are exercisable on the application` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears MEANS
        `the entity is a registered charity`
    AND `Art 14(1) the application is by the Commissioner or the Attorney General` application
    AND (   "(a)" ... `Art 14(1)(a) there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity` interp `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears
         OR "(b)" ... `Art 14(1)(b) it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the property or secure its proper application` appears)

-- The party who applies, as a role of `Legal actor`.
@ref Article 14(1) (src:407), 14(4) (src:424)
GIVEN application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14`
GIVETH A `Legal actor`
`Art 14 the party who applies` application MEANS
    CONSIDER application
    WHEN `an application by the Commissioner` THEN `the Commissioner`
    WHEN `an application by the Attorney General` THEN `the Attorney General`
    WHEN `an application by the registered charity` THEN `a registered charity`
    WHEN `an application by a person to whom the order was directed` THEN `any person`
    WHEN `an application by any other person` THEN `any person`

-- After the application, the court's permission to exercise the powers of paragraph (2) if the grounds appear to it.  The application by the
-- Commissioner is the act of whoever is acting as the Commissioner (integration point IP28).
@ref Article 14(1) (src:407)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14`
      `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14`
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 14`
`Art 14(1) the court's permission once the application is made` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears MEANS
    IF `Art 14(1) the powers of the court are exercisable on the application` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY `exercise the powers set out in Article 14(2)`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:407 | (1) The court may exercise any of the powers set out in paragraph (2) in relation to a registered charity if, on an application by the Commissioner or the Attorney General, it appears to the court –
@ref Article 14(1) (src:407)
@export The Commissioner's or the Attorney General's application to the court under Article 14(1), followed by the court's permission to exercise the powers of paragraph (2) if it appears to the court that there is or has been misconduct in the administration of the charity or that an order is necessary or desirable to protect its property, as a regulative rule; an application by anyone else opens no power under Article 14(1)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14` @desc The readings of the Law's open points; fork F5.4 decides what misconduct in the administration of the charity means
      `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is entered for the time being in the general or the restricted section of the register
      `someone is acting as the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person appointed as the Commissioner, or a designated relevant person while the appointee cannot act, is acting
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14` @desc Who applied to the court
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct in question is misconduct within Article 2(10), as it appears to the court
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)` @desc The other facts as they appear to the court
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 14`
`Art 14(1) the court may exercise the powers on the application` interp `the entity is a registered charity` `someone is acting as the Commissioner` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears MEANS
    IF     `the entity is a registered charity`
       AND `Art 14(1) the application is by the Commissioner or the Attorney General` application
       AND (   NOT (application EQUALS `an application by the Commissioner`)
            OR `someone is acting as the Commissioner`)
    THEN PARTY (`Art 14 the party who applies` application)
         MAY `apply to the court` application
         HENCE (`Art 14(1) the court's permission once the application is made` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears)
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `14(2) Any order the court sees fit`

-- Fork F5.12.  "any order that the court sees fit to remedy the misconduct, protect the property and secure its proper application".
-- Reading A (taken): an order that serves at least one of the three purposes; the "and" lists the aims of the power, and the ground in 14(1)(b) needs
-- no misconduct, so an order that only protects the property must be possible.  Reading B: an order must serve all three.
@ref Article 14(2) (src:410)
GIVEN sees IS AN `Order that the court sees fit under Article 14(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F5.12 A: the order serves at least one of the three purposes` sees MEANS
        sees's `to remedy the misconduct`
    OR  sees's `to protect the property`
    OR  sees's `to secure its proper application`

@ref Article 14(2) (src:410)
GIVEN sees IS AN `Order that the court sees fit under Article 14(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F5.12 B: the order serves all three purposes` sees MEANS
        sees's `to remedy the misconduct`
    AND sees's `to protect the property`
    AND sees's `to secure its proper application`

@ref Article 14(2) (src:410)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14`
      sees IS AN `Order that the court sees fit under Article 14(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(2) the order is one the court sees fit` interp sees MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the purposes an order under Article 14(2) must serve`
    WHEN `at least one of the three purposes` THEN `F5.12 A: the order serves at least one of the three purposes` sees
    WHEN `all three purposes` THEN `F5.12 B: the order serves all three purposes` sees

-- src:410 | (2) The court may make any order that the court sees fit to remedy the misconduct, protect the property and secure its proper application.
@ref Article 14(2) (src:410)
@export The court's permission to make any order it sees fit to remedy the misconduct, protect the property and secure its proper application, as a regulative rule: open once the powers of Article 14(1) are exercisable; whether an order serves the purposes is the court's, and whether one purpose or all three are needed is a fork
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14` @desc The readings of the Law's open points; fork F5.12 decides whether an order must serve one of the three purposes or all three, and F5.4 what misconduct in the administration means
      `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is entered for the time being in the general or the restricted section of the register
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14` @desc Who applied to the court
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct in question is misconduct within Article 2(10), as it appears to the court
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)` @desc The other facts as they appear to the court
      sees IS AN `Order that the court sees fit under Article 14(2)` @desc Whether the court sees the order fit to remedy the misconduct, to protect the property, and to secure its proper application
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 14`
`Art 14(2) the court may make any order it sees fit` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears sees MEANS
    IF     `Art 14(1) the powers of the court are exercisable on the application` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears
       AND `Art 14(2) the order is one the court sees fit` interp sees
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY `make an order that the court sees fit`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `14(3) The orders the court may make include`

-- Which person an order of each kind may be directed at, and what it must say.  "Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (2)": the nine are
-- instances of (2), so the gate of 14(1) applies to each.
@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
GIVEN order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3) the order is directed at the charity or a governor` order MEANS
    CONSIDER order's `directed at`
    WHEN `the charity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a governor of the charity` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
GIVEN order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3)(d) the order is directed at a governor or another person concerned in the management or control of the charity` order MEANS
    CONSIDER order's `directed at`
    WHEN `a governor of the charity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another person concerned in the management or control of the charity` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
GIVEN order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3)(e) the order is directed at a person who holds property on behalf of the charity or of a governor` order MEANS
    CONSIDER order's `directed at`
    WHEN `a person who holds property on behalf of the charity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a person who holds property on behalf of a governor of the charity` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
GIVEN order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3)(h) the order is directed at a governor or another person who appears to be responsible for misconduct` order MEANS
    CONSIDER order's `directed at`
    WHEN `a governor of the charity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another person who appears to be responsible for misconduct` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- src:411 | (3) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (2), the orders that the court may make include an order –
-- src:412 | (a) prohibiting (whether for a specified or indefinite period) the charity or a governor from –
-- src:413 | (i) referring to or holding out the entity as a charity or Jersey charity, or as being charitable, having charitable purposes or providing public benefit,
-- src:414 | (ii) soliciting donations from the public, as specified in the order, or
-- src:415 | (iii) taking any other action specified in the order;
-- src:416 | (b) appointing a person (whether a specified or indefinite period) to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors;
-- src:417 | (c) appointing a person to be a governor of the charity;
-- src:418 | (d) suspending or removing a governor of the charity, or any other person concerned in the management or control of the charity;
-- src:419 | (e) requiring a person (including without prejudice a person carrying on regulated financial services business) who holds property on behalf of the charity, or on behalf of a governor of the charity, not to part with the property without the court’s consent;
-- src:420 | (f) restricting the transactions that may be entered into, or the nature or amount of the payments that may be made, in the administration of the charity without the court’s consent;
-- src:421 | (g) making the administration of the charity subject to such supervision, restraint or conditions, from such time, and for such periods, as the court may specify;
-- src:422 | (h) requiring a governor who appears to the court to have engaged in any misconduct, or any other person who appears to be responsible for any misconduct, to take such steps as the court may direct to remedy the effects of the misconduct;
-- src:423 | (i) making such ancillary provision as the court thinks desirable.
@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
@export Whether an order proposed to the court is of one of the nine kinds Article 14(3) lists and can be made as proposed: a prohibition (directed at the charity or a governor, and saying what it forbids), an appointment to manage or as a governor, a suspension or removal (of a governor or another person concerned in the management), a hold on property (directed at a person who holds property for the charity or a governor), a restriction on transactions or payments, supervision or conditions, an order to remedy misconduct (directed at a governor or another person who appears to the court to be responsible), or ancillary provision
GIVEN order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)` @desc The proposed order: its kind, whom it is directed at, what a prohibition forbids, whether it appears to the court that the person engaged in or is responsible for misconduct, and its period
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3) the order is of a kind the paragraph lists` order MEANS
    CONSIDER order's kind
    WHEN `an order prohibiting the charity or a governor from holding out as a charity, soliciting donations or another specified action`
        THEN "(a)" ... `Art 14(3) the order is directed at the charity or a governor` order
             AND isJust (order's forbids)
    WHEN `an order appointing a person to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors` THEN "(b)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an order appointing a person to be a governor` THEN "(c)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an order suspending or removing a governor or another person concerned in the management`
        THEN "(d)" ... `Art 14(3)(d) the order is directed at a governor or another person concerned in the management or control of the charity` order
    WHEN `an order requiring a holder of property not to part with it without the court's consent`
        THEN "(e)" ... `Art 14(3)(e) the order is directed at a person who holds property on behalf of the charity or of a governor` order
    WHEN `an order restricting transactions or payments without the court's consent` THEN "(f)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an order subjecting the administration to supervision, restraint or conditions` THEN "(g)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an order requiring a person to take steps to remedy the effects of misconduct`
        THEN "(h)" ... `Art 14(3)(h) the order is directed at a governor or another person who appears to be responsible for misconduct` order
             AND order's `it appears to the court that the person at whom the order is directed has engaged in misconduct or is responsible for it`
    WHEN `an order making ancillary provision` THEN "(i)" ... TRUE

-- src:411 | (3) Without prejudice to the generality of paragraph (2), the orders that the court may make include an order –
-- src:412 | (a) prohibiting (whether for a specified or indefinite period) the charity or a governor from –
-- src:413 | (i) referring to or holding out the entity as a charity or Jersey charity, or as being charitable, having charitable purposes or providing public benefit,
-- src:414 | (ii) soliciting donations from the public, as specified in the order, or
-- src:415 | (iii) taking any other action specified in the order;
-- src:416 | (b) appointing a person (whether a specified or indefinite period) to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors;
-- src:417 | (c) appointing a person to be a governor of the charity;
-- src:418 | (d) suspending or removing a governor of the charity, or any other person concerned in the management or control of the charity;
-- src:419 | (e) requiring a person (including without prejudice a person carrying on regulated financial services business) who holds property on behalf of the charity, or on behalf of a governor of the charity, not to part with the property without the court’s consent;
-- src:420 | (f) restricting the transactions that may be entered into, or the nature or amount of the payments that may be made, in the administration of the charity without the court’s consent;
-- src:421 | (g) making the administration of the charity subject to such supervision, restraint or conditions, from such time, and for such periods, as the court may specify;
-- src:422 | (h) requiring a governor who appears to the court to have engaged in any misconduct, or any other person who appears to be responsible for any misconduct, to take such steps as the court may direct to remedy the effects of the misconduct;
-- src:423 | (i) making such ancillary provision as the court thinks desirable.
@ref Article 14(3) (src:411-423)
@export The court's permission to make an order of one of the kinds listed in Article 14(3), as a regulative rule (integration point IP31, which Article 17(9) lends to a deregistered entity): open only when the powers of Article 14(1) are exercisable and the order is of a kind Article 14(3) lists and can be made as proposed
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14` @desc The readings of the Law's open points
      `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is entered for the time being in the general or the restricted section of the register
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14` @desc Who applied to the court
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct in question is misconduct within Article 2(10), as it appears to the court
      appears IS A `What appears to the court under Article 14(1)` @desc The other facts as they appear to the court
      order IS AN `Order proposed under Article 14(3)` @desc The proposed order
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 14`
`Art 14(3) the court may make the order` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears order MEANS
    IF     `Art 14(1) the powers of the court are exercisable on the application` interp `the entity is a registered charity` application `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` appears
       AND `Art 14(3) the order is of a kind the paragraph lists` order
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY `make an order of a kind listed in Article 14(3)` (order's kind)
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- The period of an order: "whether for a specified or indefinite period" (14(3)(a), (b)); "from such time, and for such periods, as the court may
-- specify" (14(3)(g)).  Fork F5.9: does the first day named count as the first day of the period?  Reading A (taken): it does.
@ref Article 14(3)(a), (b), (g) (src:412) (src:416) (src:421)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14`
      named IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 14(3) the first day of the period` interp named MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whether the first day counts in a period fixed by the court`
    WHEN `the first day named is the first day of the period` THEN named
    WHEN `the period starts on the day after the first day named` THEN named PLUS 1

-- src:412 | (a) prohibiting (whether for a specified or indefinite period) the charity or a governor from –
-- src:416 | (b) appointing a person (whether a specified or indefinite period) to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors;
-- src:421 | (g) making the administration of the charity subject to such supervision, restraint or conditions, from such time, and for such periods, as the court may specify;
@ref Article 14(3)(a), (b) and (g) (src:412) (src:416) (src:421)
@export Whether an order of the court is in operation on a day, by the period it fixes: an indefinite period runs from the first day named until the order is amended or revoked; a specified period runs for the number of days it names (whether the first day named counts is a fork)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 13 and 14` @desc The readings of the Law's open points; fork F5.9 decides whether the first day named counts as the first day
      period IS A `Period of an order of the court` @desc The period the order fixes
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the question is asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(3) the order is in operation on the day` interp period `the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER period
    WHEN `an indefinite period` named
        THEN `the day` AT LEAST `Art 14(3) the first day of the period` interp named
    WHEN `a specified period` named length
        THEN     `the day` AT LEAST `Art 14(3) the first day of the period` interp named
             AND `the day` AT MOST (`Art 14(3) the first day of the period` interp named) PLUS (length MINUS 1)

§§§ `14(4) Amending or revoking an order`

-- The court may amend or revoke on the application of the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or any person to whom the order was
-- directed.  No ground is required, and the grounds of 14(1) are not.  The order must be one made under this Article.
@ref Article 14(4) (src:424)
GIVEN application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(4) the application is by one of the four` application MEANS
    CONSIDER application
    WHEN `an application by the Commissioner` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an application by the Attorney General` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an application by the registered charity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an application by a person to whom the order was directed` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an application by any other person` THEN FALSE

-- src:424 | (4) The court may, on the application of the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or any person to whom the order was directed, amend or revoke any order made under this Article.
@ref Article 14(4) (src:424)
@export The application to the court to amend or revoke an order made under Article 14, by the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or a person to whom the order was directed, followed by the court's permission to amend or revoke it, as a regulative rule; an application by anyone else, or about an order not made under Article 14, opens no power
GIVEN `the entity is a registered charity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is entered for the time being in the general or the restricted section of the register
      `someone is acting as the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person appointed as the Commissioner, or a designated relevant person while the appointee cannot act, is acting
      application IS AN `Application to the court under Article 14` @desc Who applied to the court
      `the order was made under Article 14` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order in question was made under Article 14
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 14`
`Art 14(4) the court may amend or revoke an order` `the entity is a registered charity` `someone is acting as the Commissioner` application `the order was made under Article 14` MEANS
    IF     `the order was made under Article 14`
       AND `Art 14(4) the application is by one of the four` application
       AND (   NOT (application EQUALS `an application by the Commissioner`)
            OR `someone is acting as the Commissioner`)
    THEN PARTY (`Art 14 the party who applies` application)
         MAY `apply to the court` application
         HENCE (PARTY `the court`
                MAY `amend or revoke an order made under Article 14`
                HENCE FULFILLED)
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `14(5) The powers are additional, and apply despite the constitution`

-- (a) "in addition to and not in derogation from any powers of the court under any other enactment or under customary law": the court may act if it has
-- the Article 14 power OR a power elsewhere; Article 14 takes nothing away.
@ref Article 14(5)(a) (src:426)
GIVEN `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(5)(a) the court may act under Article 14 or under another power` `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law` MEANS
        "(a)" ... `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable`
    OR  `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law`

-- (b) "despite anything in the constitution of the charity": whether a provision of the constitution forbids the order does not bear on the power.
-- The first input is deliberately not read.
@ref Article 14(5)(b) (src:427)
GIVEN `a provision of the constitution of the charity forbids the order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(5)(b) the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable despite the constitution` `a provision of the constitution of the charity forbids the order` `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` MEANS
    "(b) despite anything in the constitution of the charity" ... `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable`

-- src:425 | (5) The powers of the court under this Article apply –
-- src:426 | (a) in addition to and not in derogation from any powers of the court under any other enactment or under customary law; and
-- src:427 | (b) despite anything in the constitution of the charity.
@ref Article 14(5) (src:425-427)
@export Whether the court may act: under Article 14, which applies in addition to and not in derogation from the court's powers under any other enactment or customary law, and despite anything in the charity's constitution
GIVEN `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the powers of Article 14 are exercisable (the answer of Article 14(1))
      `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has the power it is asked to use under another enactment or under customary law
      `a provision of the constitution of the charity forbids the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a provision of the charity's constitution forbids the order; Article 14(5)(b) makes it irrelevant to the Article 14 power
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(5) the powers apply in addition to other powers and despite the constitution` `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable` `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law` `a provision of the constitution of the charity forbids the order` MEANS
    `Art 14(5)(a) the court may act under Article 14 or under another power`
        (`Art 14(5)(b) the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable despite the constitution` `a provision of the constitution of the charity forbids the order` `the powers of the court under Article 14 are exercisable`)
        `the court has the power under another enactment or under customary law`

§§§ `14(6) Without prejudice to the Commissioner's own powers`

-- "The powers of the court under this Article are without prejudice to any power of the Commissioner to take any action under this Law in respect of
-- any misconduct": the Commissioner's power is the same whether or not the court has been asked, or has acted.  The second input is deliberately not read.
-- src:428 | (6) The powers of the court under this Article are without prejudice to any power of the Commissioner to take any action under this Law in respect of any misconduct.
@ref Article 14(6) (src:428)
@export Whether the Commissioner may take action under the Law in respect of misconduct: the powers of the court under Article 14 are without prejudice to it, so the answer is the Commissioner's own power whether or not the court has acted
GIVEN `the Commissioner may take action under this Law in respect of the misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has a power under the Law (a required steps notice, deregistration) in respect of the misconduct
      `the court has acted under Article 14` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made, or has been asked to make, an order under Article 14
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 14(6) the Commissioner's own power is unaffected by the powers of the court` `the Commissioner may take action under this Law in respect of the misconduct` `the court has acted under Article 14` MEANS
    "without prejudice to any power of the Commissioner" ... `the Commissioner may take action under this Law in respect of the misconduct`
